Psoriatic Arthritis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Asymmetrical distal interphalangeal arthritis, Dactylitis (sausage digits), Psoriatic nail pitting/onycholysis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-049
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unremitting hand and foot pain, accompanied by massive swelling of individual digits, rendering the patient unable to grasp items or walk.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing an acute, functionally paralyzing flare of asymmetrical joint pain and massive, uniform digit swelling. Urgent assessment was strictly necessary to rule out acute infectious flexor tenosynovitis or a severe polyarticular septic arthritis flare, and to administer immediate, potent systemic anti-inflammatory therapy to halt profound tissue destruction and restore basic functional safety prior to daytime rheumatological review.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My index finger and second toe have swollen into massive, agonizing sausages overnight; I can't bend them, and the pain is making me sick."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, severe exacerbation of suspected inflammatory arthritis. Timeline: Intermittent, mild, asymmetrical oligoarthritis over the past year, predominantly affecting the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints. Over the last 48 hours, the patient developed severe, explosive dactylitis: the right index finger and left second toe became diffusely and uniformly swollen ("sausage digits"). Patient reports profound morning stiffness in the affected joints lasting well over an hour. Accompanied by severe, exquisite pain at the right Achilles tendon insertion (enthesitis). Patient reports profound systemic fatigue that is completely disproportionate to the degree of active joint swelling. Notes a history of dry, scaly skin patches on the elbows.
Functional Status: Severely impaired; right hand is non-functional for gripping due to the swollen index digit; walking is agonizing due to the swollen toe and heel enthesitis.
Social/Safety Concerns: Functional loss of dominant hand; high fall risk due to severe foot pain.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/86 | HR: 95 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, looking systemically fatigued, guarding the right hand and left foot.
Physical Exam: Right hand reveals severe dactylitis of the index finger: the entire digit is uniformly swollen, erythematous, and exquisitely tender, completely obliterating normal joint contours. Significant asymmetrical arthritis present in the DIP joints of the left hand. Left foot reveals identical dactylitis of the second toe. Severe, localized point tenderness and mild swelling at the right Achilles tendon insertion (enthesitis). Skin exam reveals erythematous, sharply demarcated scaly plaques on the extensor surfaces of both elbows (psoriasis). Nail exam shows prominent pitting, onycholysis, and subungual hyperkeratosis closely correlating with the involved digits. No tracking lymphangitis or fluctuant abscesses.
Environmental Observations: Patient unable to open standard medication blister packs due to dactylitis.
Medication Reconciliation: Maxed out on over-the-counter Ibuprofen with zero relief of the massive inflammatory swelling.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute, severe flare of Psoriatic Arthritis (PsA) presenting with Dactylitis and Enthesitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Infectious Flexor Tenosynovitis - Ruled out clinically by the simultaneous, multi-focal presentation (finger, toe, and heel), the presence of classic psoriatic skin/nail changes, and the lack of a primary puncture wound, though the isolated finger swelling mimics it closely.

Gout (Polyarticular) - Less likely due to the distinct DIP joint predilection, the uniform "sausage digit" presentation rather than localized joint tophi, and the presence of enthesitis and skin psoriasis.

Rheumatoid Arthritis - Ruled out clinically by the asymmetrical presentation, the specific involvement of the DIP joints (typically spared in RA), and the presence of dactylitis (rare in RA).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook, severe manifestation of active Psoriatic Arthritis, characterized by explosive dactylitis ("sausage digits"), enthesitis, and pathognomonic skin and nail changes. The critical clinical task was differentiating the massively swollen finger from a catastrophic surgical emergency like infectious flexor tenosynovitis. The multi-focal, asymmetrical presentation combined with classic dermatological signs firmly established the systemic autoimmune nature of the flare. The severity of the pain and swelling resulted in total functional loss of the dominant hand and severe difficulty walking. Urgent after-hours assessment provided crucial diagnostic validation, excluded surgical infection, and allowed for the immediate initiation of high-dose systemic corticosteroids and NSAIDs to rapidly extinguish the aggressive autoimmune tissue attack.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Intramuscular Ketorolac 30mg STAT to rapidly decrease acute systemic inflammation. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 5 days as a short bridging course to halt the severe dactylitis, alongside ongoing Naproxen 500mg BD.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Take the prescribed steroid medication exactly at 6:00 AM with food. Consequence: The medication suppresses the peak immune system attack that happens overnight, drastically reducing the severe morning stiffness and allowing you to bend your fingers to dress yourself (positive reinforcement).
Action: Elevate the affected hand and foot on thick pillows above heart level whenever resting. Consequence: Gravity assists in draining the massive inflammatory fluid pooling in the "sausage digits," rapidly reducing the tight, bursting, throbbing sensation (negative reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to a Rheumatologist for initiation of Disease-Modifying Antirheumatic Drugs (DMARDs) or Biologics.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3-5 days to monitor response to steroids and expedite rheumatology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if there is a sudden onset of a high fever with uncontrollable shivering, if a red streak starts moving up the arm from the swollen finger, or if the finger becomes completely pale and cold.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, supported by the presence of psoriasis. Blood tests (typically rheumatoid factor negative) and X-rays are required for formal rheumatological staging. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The massive swelling and "sausage digits" should improve significantly within 48-72 hours on the steroids. However, this is a destructive, chronic disease that requires powerful, long-term immune-suppressing medications to prevent permanent joint deformity.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care immediately if you develop a high fever, or if a red line starts spreading up your arm from the swollen finger.

